      "outputTokens": 1091,
      "costUsd": 0.0098925,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video ended here, with the sine curve, x in radians. Here's where a sine hump turns up. A home's solar panels make no power at sunrise, the most at noon, and none again at sunset. The energy they've collected keeps building all day, fastest at noon. Its gradient is the power, so the energy curve is a primitive of a sine curve. So which curve has gradient sine x? Cos x is the obvious guess. Test it at pi over two. Sine x is one there, so our curve should be climbing, with gradient one. But cos x is falling there, with gradient minus one. The guess has the wrong sign. So where does the minus sign go?"
    },
