      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 154,
      "outputTokens": 1223,
      "costUsd": 0.011084,
      "pronunciationText": "Check another point. At three pi over two, sine x is minus one, but cos x is climbing there, with gradient one. Everywhere, the gradient of cos x is minus sine x, the exact opposite of what we need. So turn cos x upside down. Flipping a curve upside down reverses every slope, at the same x. Now minus cos x climbs from zero to pi, exactly where sine x is positive, and falls from pi to two pi, where sine x is negative. Watch the gradient of minus cos x as a point moves along. It traces sine x exactly. Differentiate back: minus cos x has derivative minus, minus sine x, which is sine x. So the integral of sine x is minus cos x, plus C."
    },
