      "inputTokens": 95,
      "outputTokens": 727,
      "costUsd": 0.0065905,
      "pronunciationText": "Cos x is the easier one. From the derivative rules, the gradient of sine x is cos x. At zero, sine x climbs with gradient one, and cos of zero is one. At pi over two it's flat, and cos of pi over two is zero. So the integral of cos x is sine x, plus C, with no minus sign. Both rules come from derivatives that only work with x in radians, so these primitives need radians too."
    },
