      "inputTokens": 140,
      "outputTokens": 1061,
      "costUsd": 0.009619,
      "pronunciationText": "The third rule comes from tan x. In an earlier video, the derivative of tan x was sec squared x, which means one over cos squared x. So the integral of sec squared x is tan x, plus C. Look at the graphs. Cos squared x is at most one, so sec squared x is never less than one, and tan x climbs all along each piece. At pi over four, its gradient is two, and sec squared of pi over four is two. But tan x has asymptotes, wherever cos x is zero, such as at pi over two. So, like the natural log of mod x last video, the answer works on one piece at a time."
    },
