      "pronunciationText": "What if the inside isn't linear? Find the integral of minus two x, times sine of x squared plus one. The inside is x squared plus one, and its derivative, two x, is out the front. And minus sine is the derivative of cos. So this is the chain rule in reverse, and the answer is cos of x squared plus one, plus C. Differentiate back: minus sine of x squared plus one, times two x. That's what we started with. The inner derivative has to be there. Cos of x squared, on its own, has no two x out the front, so these rules can't integrate it."
