      "inputTokens": 154,
      "outputTokens": 1159,
      "costUsd": 0.010508,
      "pronunciationText": "The same reverse chain rule works for cos and sec squared. Find the integral of three x squared, times cos of x cubed. The inside is x cubed, and three x squared is its derivative, out the front. So the answer is sine of x cubed, plus C. Now a harder-looking one: cos x, times sec squared of sine x. The inside is sine x, and its derivative, cos x, is out the front, so the answer is tan of sine x, plus C. What about the asymptotes? Tan needs its inside to avoid pi over two. But sine x stays between minus one and one, and pi over two is about one point five seven, so it never gets there. This answer works for every x."
